# name: test/sql/container_vfs.test
# description: docx, odt and epub read through DuckDB's filesystem, not just the local disk (#120)
# group: [sql]

#
# THIS FILE GIVES NO CI PROTECTION, AND THAT IS STATED FIRST SO NOBODY READS IT AS
# A GATE. httpfs is out-of-tree in duckdb v1.5.6, this repo's
# extension_config.cmake loads only panduck, and declaring httpfs would need
# `openssl` + `curl` in vcpkg.json -- whose blast radius includes the Wasm builds,
# which is why an earlier attempt at exactly that was reverted (d2558ba). So
# `require httpfs` below SKIPS this whole file on every CI runner.
#
# What it is instead: developer-facing coverage that RUNS for anyone with httpfs
# installed, plus an HONEST DECLARATION. scripts/check_test_skips.py predicts a
# `require <ext>` skip since #127, and this file's (file, reason) pair is in that
# script's DECLARED map with this reason -- so the guard stays green by AGREEING
# that this file skips, rather than by not noticing. Before #127 a file like this
# one went missing while the guard printed "4 predicted skip(s), 4 declared. OK".
#
# THE MECHANISM IS COVERED HERMETICALLY ELSEWHERE. libpanduck's
# test/test_byte_source.cpp opens real .docx and .epub archives through a
# MemorySource and pins member byte lengths, which is the same ByteSource path
# this file exercises with a FileHandleSource on the far end. That test runs
# everywhere and needs no network. This file covers the one thing it cannot: that
# the extension's three container tails actually open through
# FileSystem::GetFileSystem(context).
#
# THE URL IS PINNED TO A COMMIT SHA, not to a branch, so this file keeps testing
# the same bytes after main moves. b97f10e is the merge of #120 step 1, where
# these three fixtures are present and byte-identical to the local ones.
#
# MEASURED BEFORE THE FIX, on the same build with httpfs loaded -- all three
# readers failed, and failed MISLEADINGLY, because a path handed to miniz could
# only ever be a local one:
#
#   IO Error: read_docx_blocks: not a readable ZIP archive: https://raw.github...
#   IO Error: read_odt_blocks:  not a readable ZIP archive: https://raw.github...
#   IO Error: read_epub_blocks: not a readable ZIP archive: https://raw.github...
#

require httpfs

require panduck

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ROW COUNTS ARE THE LOCAL ONES. That is the assertion: the same fixture read
# over HTTPS yields exactly what it yields off the disk, so nothing about going
# through a handle changed what the readers see.
#
#   test/fixtures/rich.docx    10 rows   (docx_reader.test reads it locally)
#   test/fixtures/rich.odt     12 rows
#   test/fixtures/pandoc.epub  24 rows
#
# Counts rather than content, because content is already asserted in depth by the
# three per-format test files against the identical local fixtures; repeating that
# here would duplicate coverage that runs everywhere with coverage that does not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_docx_blocks('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/teaguesterling/duckdb_panduck/b97f10e9c0a12a61c3936323ddff7c725e598f2f/test/fixtures/rich.docx');
----
10

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_odt_blocks('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/teaguesterling/duckdb_panduck/b97f10e9c0a12a61c3936323ddff7c725e598f2f/test/fixtures/rich.odt');
----
12

# EPUB IS THE ONE WORTH READING REMOTELY. A book is a SPINE of members reached
# through META-INF/container.xml and the OPF, so this count is the product of
# dozens of positional reads against one open handle rather than of a single
# whole-file fetch -- which is why these readers take a handle instead of reusing
# readers::ReadFileThroughVFS.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM read_epub_blocks('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/teaguesterling/duckdb_panduck/b97f10e9c0a12a61c3936323ddff7c725e598f2f/test/fixtures/pandoc.epub');
----
24

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AND THE ERROR CONVERGED TOO. A URL that does not exist is "file not found" --
# the same wording readers::ReadFileThroughVFS has given the other ten readers
# since #122, because both now go through readers::OpenFileThroughVFS. Before
# this change a missing remote file and a corrupt local one gave the SAME message
# ("not a readable ZIP archive"), which named neither.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

statement error
SELECT * FROM read_docx_blocks('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/teaguesterling/duckdb_panduck/b97f10e9c0a12a61c3936323ddff7c725e598f2f/test/fixtures/does_not_exist.docx');
----
file not found
